course: dictionary
lesson: 4401
title: The hajj
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The hajj is a pilgrimage, which means a long journey made for religious reasons. Muslims go to the holy city of Mecca in Saudi Arabia. When you read «hajj» with a capital H, it names this journey.
explain: Context helps here: «The Five Pillars are: the declaration of faith, prayer five times a day, giving to the poor (zakat), fasting in Ramadan and the pilgrimage to Mecca (Hajj).» The list shows that the hajj is one of the main duties of Muslims, and the brackets show that Hajj is another name for the pilgrimage to Mecca.
explain: Look at the phrases «go on hajj», «make the hajj» and «perform hajj». They all mean the same thing: to travel to Mecca on the pilgrimage. You may also see the spellings hadj and haj.
fact: The hajj means the pilgrimage to Mecca in Saudi Arabia.
fact: Every adult Muslim who is healthy and can afford it should make the hajj at least once in their life.
fact: The hajj is one of the Five Pillars of Islam, the main duties of Muslims.
fact: On the hajj, Muslims wear simple white clothes called ihram, walk around the Kaaba seven times and pray at Mount Arafat.
fact: The hajj shows that all believers are equal before God.
exercise: What is the hajj? | A festival of lights held in the autumn | A kind of bread eaten at Eid | *A pilgrimage to the holy city of Mecca | A football match in Saudi Arabia
exercise: In «...the pilgrimage to Mecca (Hajj).», what do the brackets show? | The name of a holy mountain | The number of pillars in Islam | *Another name for the pilgrimage to Mecca | A kind of prayer said at home
exercise: What do Muslims wear on the hajj? | Heavy winter coats | School uniform | *Simple white clothes called ihram | Bright coloured robes covered in gold
test: Which of these is one of the Five Pillars of Islam? | *Hajj | Ihram | Eid al-Adha | Kaaba
test: Who should make the hajj? | Only Muslim kings | Every child in the world | *Every adult Muslim who is healthy and can afford it, at least once | Anyone who wants a holiday abroad
exam: What does the hajj show about believers? | *They are all equal before God | Some believers are more important than others | Only rich people can be believers | Believers never travel anywhere
